Let $M$ be a subspace of a Hilbert space $H$, let $T$ be any operator in $B[H]$, and take any $y\in M^\perp$. If $Tx\in M$ whenever $x\in M$, then $(x;T^*y)= (Tx;y)=0$ for every $x\in M$ so that $T^*y\perp M$. Then $T^*y\in M^\perp$ for every $y\in M^\perp$. Conclusion: $T(M)\subseteq M$ implies $T^*(M^\perp)\subseteq M^\perp$. Conversely, since the above implication holds for every $T\in B[H]$, it follows that $T^*(M^\perp)\subseteq M^\perp$ implies $T^{**}(M^{\perp\perp})\subseteq M^{\perp\perp}$. But $T^{**}=T$ and $M^{\perp\perp} =M^{-} =M$ (Propositions 5.15 and 5.65(c)). Thus $T^*(M^\perp)\subseteq M^\perp$ implies $T(M)\subseteq M$. Finally, note that $\{0\}
eq M
eq H$ if and only if $\{0\}
eq M^\perp
eq H$ (Proposition 5.15).

